1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:05,252
When a robot talks, its face should move with what it says.

2
00:00:05,952 --> 00:00:09,449
Try it yourself: the live demo runs in your browser.

3
00:00:09,799 --> 00:00:17,035
LLM-driven robot faces wait for the whole reply, then show one emotion label for the entire turn.

4
00:00:17,285 --> 00:00:24,184
Emo-H2A removes the label, and drives the face from the model's hidden states, token by token.

5
00:00:24,434 --> 00:00:29,493
Affective axes are built once from the model's own vocabulary, with no training.

6
00:00:29,743 --> 00:00:36,364
At every token, the hidden state is projected onto valence, arousal, and dominance.

7
00:00:36,614 --> 00:00:42,957
A fuzzy mapping turns this continuous affect into smoothly blended expressions across 29 actuators.

8
00:00:43,207 --> 00:00:48,448
The face follows the turns inside a reply, and first moves in under one second.

9
00:01:15,027 --> 00:01:18,845
Here is the same reply, driven five different ways.

10
00:01:31,195 --> 00:01:36,202
How soon does the face react? EmoManifold moves in under one second.

11
00:01:36,716 --> 00:01:40,342
Turn-level systems wait for the whole reply, then for a label.

12
00:01:42,142 --> 00:01:47,059
Xpress-Story plans the whole reply first: over ninety seconds.

13
00:01:49,845 --> 00:01:58,171
Emo-H2A needs no training, follows the reply token by token, and observers preferred it in 83.5% of trials.

14
00:01:59,021 --> 00:02:02,148
Let the face move with what the robot says.

